#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/id #uuid "5a52cc3b-4e4b-5b66-b1e0-589e3dd806e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4219" :question/problem "Evaluate the series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty\\left(\\dfrac 2 3\\right)^n$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2$. However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\displaystyle \\sum ar^n$.\n\nIn this case, the first term is $a_2$, and so the sum to infinity $S_\\infty$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_2}{1-r}, \\qquad \\mid r \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= (\\frac{2}{3})^{2} = \\frac{4}{9} \\\\\na_{3} &= (\\frac{2}{3})^{3} = \\frac{8}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_3}{a_2} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{8}{27}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{9}\\right)} = \\dfrac 2 3\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 2)^(∞)(\\frac{2}{3})^{n} &= \\frac{a_{2}}{1 - r} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4}{9}))/((1 - \\frac{2}{3})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4}{9}))/((\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cdot 3}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "90ac2db3-26c1-533d-8a0d-edceba8d565f" :question/math-academy-id "e-685" :question/problem "Evaluate the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 2(0.5)^{n}$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\displaystyle \\sum ar^n$.\n\nThe sum to infinity $S_\\infty$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_1}{1-r}, \\qquad \\mid r \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\na_1 = 2(0.5)^{1} = 1\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{2(0.5)^2}{2(0.5)^1} = 0.5\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)2(0.5)^{n} &= \\frac{a_{1}}{1 - r} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 - 0.5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "6126f9d5-f163-5167-b524-710146791e06" :question/math-academy-id "e-686" :question/problem "Given that $a_1=-2$ and $\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n}} = -0.4$ for all integers $n \\geq 1$, find the value of $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity $S_\\infty$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_1}{1-r}, \\qquad \\mid r \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1=-2\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n}} = -0.4\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n} &= \\frac{a_{1}}{1 - r} \\\\\n&= (-2)/(1 - (-0.4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2}{1.4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{10}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092108 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "fff2b9cd-5963-501d-b50a-848850e88a2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-687" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=3}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2n}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3$. In this case, the first term is $a_3$, and so the sum to infinity $S_\\infty$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_3}{1-r}, \\qquad \\mid r \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\na_3 = \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2(3)} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{6} = 5\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{7}\n$$\n\n$$\na_4 = \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2(4)} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{8} = 5\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_4}{a_3} = \\dfrac{5\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{9}}{5\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{7}} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 3)^(∞)\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{2})^{2n} &= \\frac{a_{3}}{1 - r} \\\\\n&= (5(\\frac{1}{2})^{7})/((1 - \\frac{1}{4})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{5}{128}))/((\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{5}{128})(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\\n&= (5)/(3(32)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{96}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5cec570-331b-5761-a994-262c8147d70e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124575" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=10$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1-(-0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1+0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,000}{125} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf1f4594-bcb0-53bd-9558-7de1afb93bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-124577" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-14$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a73c4b15-30e3-596c-8756-b257e8707bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089412} {:answer/id #uuid "aedd6e37-5993-58b3-a481-c0ddee6b3a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089413} {:answer/id #uuid "f3690cdb-243d-5845-a43d-208bfd74168b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089414} {:answer/id #uuid "0d097fd3-bab5-5639-a0b4-2c8c444dcda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089415} {:answer/id #uuid "645b6acc-489a-5763-a396-c2d45c7bec62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089416}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fb38ad5b-4ed2-5c45-954e-108522d434b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089411}] :question/id #uuid "7322fdcb-2ecd-5661-80da-ace9d4087c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-13580" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3(0.5)^{4n}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7282c2a-54bb-5833-928b-ed4ac66fa331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7282c2a-54bb-5833-928b-ed4ac66fa331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232984} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78186cd-42ea-5ba4-ba88-adb813f8e3ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ccdb37f-3fc9-5a29-ba10-0b973b38de6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-350648" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{\\,n-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{3-2}=6(-\\frac{1}{5})=-\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\n$$\n{a}_{4}=6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{4-2}=6(\\frac{1}{25})=\\frac{6}{25}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{25})}{(-\\frac{6}{5})}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{\\,n-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{6}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{6}{5})}{(\\frac{6}{5})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9984b00a-dafa-5ce2-809f-259e529e40d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090767} {:answer/id #uuid "d64c5fde-9796-599e-bfc7-dd3bf4d8b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090768} {:answer/id #uuid "1f19ab89-a3d1-5650-91e2-ecb1d0b83c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{180}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090769} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9b93e6-6c4e-59cc-916a-c4ff3ca8b6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090770} {:answer/id #uuid "6a01f4cc-d02e-5522-be0a-92c3148d3b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090771}] :answer-field/id #uuid "21429259-d19b-5015-9e9b-1799bacbedec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090766}] :question/id #uuid "2ae9971f-bfdd-59a5-a032-19bc1cd6f90a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50484" :question/problem "Given that $a_{1} =-18$ and $\\frac{a_{n + 1}}{a_{n}} =-0.9$ for all integers $n \\ge 1$, find the value of $∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid 